    "math_academy_id": "q-49054",
    "knowledge_point_id": "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/47849c87c3c8cf9008b6531e740c6fc57058905cf17e31e4c93c479f337f0fbd.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-2+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?",
    "worked_solution": "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{({e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
